  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 129 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSaturday through Monday\nAssessment...a certainty (>98%) of high heat and humidity. Low\n(<20%) confidence regarding possible storms\n\nHeat and humidity will surge into the area as the heat dome builds \nnorth into the Plains. Taking into account the warm and moist bias \nof the model consensus, dew points will slowly climb through the 60s \non Saturday with low and mid 70s dew points Sunday and Monday. With \nprogged temperatures eventually reaching 90 to 95 Sunday/Monday, \nheat indices at or exceeding 100 are expected Sunday into Monday.\n\nThe wild card in all this will be the organized nocturnal storms \nmoving around the edge of the heat dome. The activity will move \nacross Minnesota into Wisconsin and eventually turn south toward \nIllinois. When/where they turn south will be the key to whether or \nnot parts of the area get side-swiped by the storm complexes and \npotential diurnal development in the afternoon. We'll be dealing \nwith non-linear processes which are not handled well by the models. \nStorm development Friday night into Saturday will influence the \ndevelopment of storms the following night et cetera.\n\nThus while the potential for storms is low, if storms occur the \nimpacts could be significant; one is strong to severe storms, the \nother is potentially much lower temperatures for parts of the area \nSunday and Monday.\n\nGiven this we will be hoisting an excessive heat watch for areas \nalong and west of the Mississippi and west central Illinois. These \nheadlines may or may not need to be expanded further east of the \nMississippi.\n\nMonday night through Thursday\nAssessment...high (>80%) confidence of continued heat and humidity\n\nStarting Monday night and continuing the remainder of the week, the \nheat dome and associated upper level ridge slowly de-amplifies. A \nweak surface high will briefly turn winds around to the north and \neast Monday night through Wednesday that will slightly lower \ntemperatures. However, heat indices in the mid and upper 90s will \nstill be common.\n\nWednesday night into Thursday the various models bring a very weak \ndisturbance through the area. Given the weak forcing this \ndisturbance may or may not produce some convection. Several model \nsolutions are dry but some of their ensemble members generate some \nlight QPF. Thus while the model consensus has a 20 percent chance of \nrain, most of the area will remain dry.",
